Field Value Author / Reviewers Platform team / Checkout, Ops, SRE Status Approved Related ADRs ADR-011 Serviceability consistency levels <p>1. Context &amp; Problem. Serviceability answers are read on every app open, address change, and checkout. The current 6-hour cache caused undeliverable paid orders after a store closure. We need fast reads with explicit, bounded staleness.</p> <p>2. Goals / Non-Goals.</p> <ul> <li>Goals: fast lookups; per-use-case consistency; operator changes take effect predictably; observable staleness.</li> <li>Non-goals: polygon/geofence-level serviceability (future, Ch 47); slot capacity (owned by <code>slot-service</code>).</li> </ul> <p>3. Requirements.</p> ID Requirement FR-1 Lookup by pincode → serviceable flag + store IDs FR-2 Ops can activate/pause/retire an area; changes are audited FR-3 Checkout rejects non-serviceable pincodes NFR-1 Browse lookup p99 ≤ 10 ms in-service NFR-2 Fresh (checkout) lookup p99 ≤ 50 ms, timeout 80 ms NFR-3 Staleness: browse ≤ 60 s; checkout ≤ 10 s after operator change NFR-4 Availability 99.95 % for reads; reads survive DB outage (stale-serving) NFR-5 Sustain 6,000 lookups/s at peak <p>4. Capacity estimates.</p> <ul> <li>Lookups: 2 M DAU × 5 lookups/day = 10 M/day → ≈116/s average. Evening peak 5× → ~600/s. Flash sale 20× in the first minute → ≈2,300/s. Design target 6,000/s for headroom.</li> <li>Data: ~8,000 serviceable pincodes × ~2 stores × ~150 bytes ≈ 2.4 MB in memory. Trivially fits on every pod.</li> <li>Fresh (checkout) checks: 400 K orders/day ≈ 5/s average; festival day 1.5 M orders with a 20× first-minute spike ≈ 350/s. A primary-key lookup at that rate is negligible load for PostgreSQL.</li> <li>Writes: tens per day. Read:write is roughly 10⁶ : 1.</li> </ul> <p>What the estimate tells us</p> <p>The dataset is tiny and read-dominated. The right design is a full in-memory replica on every instance, not a distributed cache or a DB call per request. The hard part isn't scale. It's freshness and failure behaviour.</p> <p>5. Proposed design. Source of truth: PostgreSQL table <code>serviceable_area</code>. Every read pod holds an immutable snapshot. Refresh happens (a) on <code>serviceability.area.changed.v1</code> events (published via transactional outbox), typically within 1–2 s, and (b) as a full reload every 30 s as a safety net. Requests carry <code>consistency=browse|fresh</code>. Browse is served from the snapshot. Fresh (checkout only, ≈ 350/s peak) always does a primary-key read against PostgreSQL. The split works because the high-volume path tolerates staleness and the low-volume path needs correctness.</p> <p>5.2 API.</p> <pre><code>GET /api/v1/serviceability/{pincode}                      → public, browse
GET /internal/serviceability/{pincode}?consistency=fresh  → service-to-service
PUT /admin/v1/serviceability/areas/{pincode}/stores/{storeId}  {status: ACTIVE|PAUSED|RETIRED}
</code></pre> <p>5.3 Data model.</p> <pre><code>CREATE TABLE serviceable_area (
    pincode      CHAR(6)      NOT NULL,
    store_id     VARCHAR(32)  NOT NULL,
    status       VARCHAR(16)  NOT NULL,       -- PLANNED | ACTIVE | PAUSED | RETIRED
    version      BIGINT       NOT NULL,       -- global monotonic, from sequence
    updated_by   VARCHAR(64)  NOT NULL,
    updated_at   TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    PRIMARY KEY (pincode, store_id)
);
CREATE SEQUENCE serviceability_version_seq;
CREATE INDEX idx_area_active ON serviceable_area (pincode) WHERE status = 'ACTIVE';
</code></pre> <p>7. Alternatives considered.</p> Option Pros Cons Verdict DB query per request Always fresh DB on critical path for 6 K/s; outage = total outage Rejected for browse; used for fresh (low volume) Redis shared cache Shared freshness Extra network hop and dependency for a 2.4 MB dataset Rejected: adds a failure mode, no benefit at this size Long TTL cache (status quo) Fast, simple Unbounded business risk Rejected: caused the incident In-memory snapshot + event refresh + DB fallback for fresh Fast, bounded staleness, survives DB outage for browse More moving parts; needs staleness metrics Chosen <p>8. Failure modes. Kafka down → 30 s full reload still bounds browse staleness. DB down → browse serves last snapshot (alert when age &gt; 90 s); fresh checks fail closed after the 80 ms timeout (see ADR). Pod starts with no snapshot → readiness probe fails until first load.</p> <p>10. Observability. <code>serviceability.snapshot.age.seconds</code> (gauge, alert &gt; 90 s), <code>serviceability.snapshot.version</code>, <code>serviceability.lookup{consistency,result}</code> (counter/timer), <code>serviceability.fresh.db</code> (timer, with <code>outcome</code> tag).</p> <p>11. Rollout. Feature flag <code>serviceability.fresh-check.enabled</code> in checkout; shadow-compare old vs new answers for 1 week; then enforce.</p> <p>14. Decision (ADR-011 summary). Consistency is chosen per use case: browse gets bounded staleness (≤ 60 s) served from memory; checkout gets a fresh read from the source of truth. When a fresh check can't be answered in time, checkout fails closed (rejects with a retryable error). An undeliverable paid order costs more than a retried checkout.</p>

Offset (<code>?page=5&amp;size=20</code>) Cursor, opaque (<code>?cursor=eyJ0...</code>) backed by keyset SQL <code>ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20 OFFSET 80</code> <code>WHERE (created_at, id) &lt; (:ts, :id) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT 21</code> Cost for deep pages O(offset): the DB reads and discards 80, 800, 80,000 rows O(limit) with an index, regardless of depth Stability while data changes New rows shift pages, causing duplicates or skipped items Stable: each page continues after the last item seen Jump to page N ✅ ❌ (only next/prev) Total count Easy (but <code>COUNT(*)</code> is expensive at scale) Usually omitted or approximated Use for Admin tables with small data, "page 3 of 7" UIs Feeds, order history, APIs, infinite scroll, exports <p>The L1 run in §5 shows the stability property: a new order that arrives between page 1 and page 2 doesn't shift items into page 2.</p> <pre><code>2. page 1:  [ord_5, ord_4]
   page 2:  [ord_3, ord_2]  (no duplicate, new order not shifted in)
   page 3:  [ord_1]  next=null
</code></pre> <p>Keyset rules:</p> <ol> <li>The sort key must be unique, so add a tiebreaker (<code>id</code>).</li> <li>There must be a composite index matching the sort: <code>(customer_id, created_at DESC, id DESC)</code>.</li> <li>The cursor is opaque to clients (base64url, and ideally signed), so you can change its internals later.</li> <li>Fetch <code>limit + 1</code> rows to know whether there's a next page, without a <code>COUNT</code>.</li> <li>Put a maximum limit on page size (for example 50).</li> </ol> <pre><code>{
  "items": [ { "id": "ord_5", "status": "PLACED" }, { "id": "ord_4", "status": "DELIVERED" } ],
  "page": { "limit": 2, "nextCursor": "MjAyNi0wOS0yNlQxMDowMDowNFp8b3JkXzQ" },
  "_links": { "next": { "href": "/api/v1/orders?limit=2&amp;cursor=MjAyNi0wOS0yNlQxMDowMDowNFp8b3JkXzQ" } }
}
</code></pre>

Field Value Author / Reviewers Order team / Mobile, Web, Seller platform, Security, API guild Status Approved Related ADRs ADR-020 Idempotency keys mandatory on mutating customer APIs; ADR-021 URI major versioning + additive evolution <p>1. Context &amp; Problem. Duplicate orders and payments from client retries (§1.1). Offset pagination on order history degraded as heavy users' histories grew. There were no conditional updates, and error responses were inconsistent across services.</p> <p>2. Goals / Non-Goals.</p> <ul> <li>Goals: retry-safe creation and actions; stable, fast pagination; concurrency-safe state changes; one error model; a contract-first spec with SDKs.</li> <li>Non-goals: checkout orchestration internals (Ch 13, 46); GraphQL (revisit per §2.11).</li> </ul> <p>3. Requirements.</p> ID Requirement FR-1 Create an order from a cart (<code>POST /api/v1/orders</code>) FR-2 List my orders, newest first (<code>GET /api/v1/orders</code>) FR-3 Get an order (<code>GET /api/v1/orders/{id}</code>) FR-4 Cancel an order before packing (<code>POST /api/v1/orders/{id}/cancel</code>) NFR-1 Every mutating call requires <code>Idempotency-Key</code>; a duplicate never creates a second order (verified under concurrency) NFR-2 List p99 ≤ 150 ms at any page depth NFR-3 Cancel requires <code>If-Match</code>; stale → 412 NFR-4 All errors are <code>application/problem+json</code> with a stable <code>code</code> NFR-5 No breaking change within v1; enforced by CI diff <p>4. Capacity estimates.</p> Item Estimate Create attempts (festival peak, Ch 02) ~850/s Idempotency records 1.5 M orders/day × ~1.5 attempts × ~1.2 KB stored response ≈ 2.7 GB/day; 24 h TTL → &lt; 3 GB live List calls ~2 per order-history screen visit; ~3 K/s peak, served by index <code>(customer_id, created_at DESC, id DESC)</code> Get calls ~5 K/s at peak (tracking screen fallback polling, BFF) Seller webhooks ~1.5 M/day order events × ~1.1 (cancellations) → ~20/s average, ~400/s peak <p>5.2 API contract (excerpt).</p> <pre><code>openapi: 3.1.0
info: { title: Orders API, version: 1.4.0 }
paths:
  /api/v1/orders:
    post:
      operationId: createOrder
      parameters:
        - { name: Idempotency-Key, in: header, required: true, schema: { type: string, format: uuid } }
      requestBody:
        required: true
        content:
          application/json:
            schema: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/CreateOrderRequest' }
      responses:
        '201':
          description: Created (or replayed)
          headers:
            Location: { schema: { type: string } }
            ETag: { schema: { type: string } }
          content: { application/json: { schema: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/Order' } } }
        '409': { $ref: '#/components/responses/Problem' }
        '422': { $ref: '#/components/responses/Problem' }
    get:
      operationId: listOrders
      parameters:
        - { name: limit, in: query, schema: { type: integer, minimum: 1, maximum: 50, default: 20 } }
        - { name: cursor, in: query, schema: { type: string } }
        - { name: status, in: query, schema: { type: array, items: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/OrderStatus' } } }
      responses:
        '200': { content: { application/json: { schema: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/OrderPage' } } } }
  /api/v1/orders/{orderId}/cancel:
    post:
      operationId: cancelOrder
      parameters:
        - { name: orderId, in: path, required: true, schema: { type: string } }
        - { name: If-Match, in: header, required: true, schema: { type: string } }
        - { name: Idempotency-Key, in: header, required: true, schema: { type: string, format: uuid } }
      responses:
        '200': { content: { application/json: { schema: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/Order' } } } }
        '409': { $ref: '#/components/responses/Problem' }
        '412': { $ref: '#/components/responses/Problem' }
        '428': { $ref: '#/components/responses/Problem' }
components:
  schemas:
    OrderStatus:
      type: string
      enum: [PLACED, CONFIRMED, PACKED, OUT_FOR_DELIVERY, DELIVERED, CANCELLED]
      description: Clients MUST treat unknown values as "OTHER" (new statuses may be added in v1).
</code></pre> <p>5.3 Data model.</p> <pre><code>CREATE TABLE idempotency_record (
    scope          VARCHAR(64)  NOT NULL,     -- customer id or API client id
    idem_key       VARCHAR(64)  NOT NULL,
    request_hash   CHAR(64)     NOT NULL,     -- SHA-256 hex of method + path + canonical body
    status         VARCHAR(16)  NOT NULL,     -- IN_PROGRESS | COMPLETED
    locked_until   TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL,     -- takeover point if the owner crashes
    response_code  INT,
    response_body  TEXT,
    resource_id    VARCHAR(64),
    created_at     TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    expires_at     TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (scope, idem_key)
);
CREATE INDEX idx_idem_expiry ON idempotency_record (expires_at);

CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_keyset ON orders (customer_id, created_at DESC, id DESC);
</code></pre> <p>7. Alternatives considered.</p> Option Pros Cons Verdict Rely on client not retrying Nothing to build Networks retry for you (proxies, SDKs, users) Rejected Business dedupe only ("one order per cart") Simple A cart can legitimately be re-ordered; doesn't cover cancel/refund actions Complementary Idempotency in Redis Fast Harder to make atomic with the order write; eviction loses keys Rejected for money flows Idempotency table in the same DB as orders, same transaction for completion Atomic with the business write; durable Extra write per request Chosen Offset pagination Simple, page numbers O(offset), unstable pages Admin UIs only Keyset with opaque signed cursor O(limit), stable No "jump to page N" Chosen <p>8. Failure modes. A pod crashes mid-request: the record stays <code>IN_PROGRESS</code> until <code>locked_until</code> (30 s). The next retry takes over and checks whether an order already exists for the key's <code>resource_id</code> before re-executing. DB unavailable: 503 with a retryable problem, and the client retries with the same key. Clock skew between pods: <code>locked_until</code> uses DB time (<code>now()</code>), not pod time.</p> <p>9. Security. Keys are scoped to the authenticated customer. Cursors are HMAC-signed (tampering returns 400). Order ids are opaque (no enumeration). Other customers' orders return 404. Problem responses never include internal details.</p> <p>11. Rollout. <code>Idempotency-Key</code> is optional in app version N (logged when missing), then required from version N+2 (428 for older apps, with a force-upgrade prompt). The keyset list ships as a new <code>cursor</code> param while <code>page</code> stays for 2 releases.</p> <p>14. Decision (ADR-020 summary). All mutating customer and seller APIs require an <code>Idempotency-Key</code>, scoped per client, fingerprinted, stored in the same database and transaction as the business effect, and retained for 24 hours. In-progress duplicates get 409 with <code>Retry-After</code>, and mismatched bodies get 422.</p>

